      "text": "To find the greatest common divisor (GCD) of 12 and 20, we can use the Euclidean algorithm. The Euclidean algorithm involves repeated division and taking remainders until we reach a remainder of 0. The last non-zero remainder is the GCD.\n\n1. Divide 20 by 12 and find the remainder:\n   \\[\n   20 \\div 12 = 1 \\quad \\text{remainder} \\quad 8\n   \\]\n   So, \\(20 = 12 \\cdot 1 + 8\\).\n\n2. Now, divide 12 by 8 and find the remainder:\n   \\[\n   12 \\div 8 = 1 \\quad \\text{remainder} \\quad 4\n   \\]\n   So, \\(12 = 8 \\cdot 1 + 4\\).\n\n3. Next, divide 8 by 4 and find the remainder:\n   \\[\n   8 \\div 4 = 2 \\quad \\text{remainder} \\quad 0\n   \\]\n   So, \\(8 = 4 \\cdot 2 + 0\\).\n\nSince the remainder is now 0, the divisor at this step, which is 4, is the greatest common divisor of 12 and 20.\n\nTherefore, the greatest common divisor of 12 and 20 is \\(\\boxed{4}\\).",
